### What is required at each level:

| Level | Requirements | Achievable? |
|:---|:---|:---|
| **Workshop** | Incremental algorithm + 2 baselines + basic experiments | Yes, easily |
| **Second-tier conference** (CIKM, SDM, WSDM) | Formally defined adaptive criteria + ≥3 baselines (including DynaMo/TILES) + NMI/ARI on synthetic + real scalability + ablation | Yes, with effort |
| **First-tier conference** (KDD, WWW, NeurIPS) | Theoretical result (even partial) OR completely novel objective (Surprise) OR most comprehensive empirical study to date + clear differentiation from HIT-Leiden | Hard but possible |
| **Journal** (TKDE, TKDD) | Extended version of conference paper with additional experiments and analysis | Yes, if conference version exists |

### Strongest Publication Angle

**"Resolution-Limit-Free Incremental Community Detection via Surprise Optimization"** — submitted to KDD or WWW.

This immediately differentiates from ALL existing work because no existing incremental method addresses the resolution limit. The practical motivation is clear: all existing methods (Louvain, Leiden, HIT-Leiden, DynaMo) use modularity, which cannot detect communities smaller than √(2m) nodes.

### Weakest Publication Angle

"Incremental community detection with adaptive expansion" — without theory and without differentiation from HIT-Leiden/Dynamic Frontier. This is likely rejected at any venue above workshop level.

